The approach: use **nested loops** to compare every element of the first array with every element of the second array. To avoid printing duplicates, we check whether the element already appeared earlier in the first array.

## Python Implementation

```python
def find_common_elements(arr1, arr2):
    n1, n2 = len(arr1), len(arr2)

    for i in range(n1):
        # Skip if this element already appeared in arr1 (avoid duplicate output)
        already_seen = False
        for k in range(i):
            if arr1[k] == arr1[i]:
                already_seen = True
                break
        if already_seen:
            continue

        # Check if arr1[i] exists in arr2
        for j in range(n2):
            if arr1[i] == arr2[j]:
                print(arr1[i], end=" ")
                break
    print()


